1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,000
Hi! In this video, we'll look at how you can complement your manual testing with Burp Scanner.

2
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:18,000
In addition to its automated testing capabilities, Burp Scanner can also be a powerful tool in your manual testing workflow,

3
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:25,000
enabling you to investigate items or areas of interest without having to scan your entire application.

4
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:30,000
For example, you can use Scanner to perform targeted scans on specific requests,

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:34,000
which you can then investigate further using Burp's manual testing tools.

6
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:40,000
If you'd like to follow along, you can use our deliberately vulnerable website, ginandjuice.shop.

7
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:43,000
First, we'll look at scanning a specific request.

8
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:51,000
To do this, go to Proxy, Intercept, click on Open Browser, and explore your target application.

9
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:02,000
You can now go to the Proxy History tab to take a closer look at the requests that have been sent.

10
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:12,000
When you spot a request of interest, you can right-click and choose to Scan, Do Passive Scan, or Do Active Scan.

11
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:17,000
Scan will allow you to adjust the scan's configuration before it starts.

12
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:24,000
Do Passive Scan will run an audit-only scan of the unmodified request and the response it received.

13
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:30,000
Do Active Scan will run an audit-only scan using the default configuration.

14
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:36,000
This will involve sending modified requests containing payloads to probe for additional vulnerabilities.

15
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:42,000
Once you've started the scan, if you switch to the Dashboard tab, you can monitor the progress and view the results.

16
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:55,000
Burp also gives you the option to manually define insertion points and limit the audit phase of the scan to use only those insertion points,

17
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:01,000
so you can focus on specific inputs that you want to test and reduce the number of requests required.

18
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:08,000
This also lets you scan a request using inputs that Burp Scanner would normally ignore, such as custom header values.

19
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:18,000
To manually define and scan one or more insertion points in one request, right-click the relevant request and select Send to Intruder.

20
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:24,000
In the Message Editor, highlight the parts of the requests you want to use as your insertion points.

21
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:30,000
Click Add to define each one. We'll just set one here for the category.

22
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:36,000
If you define multiple insertion points, Burp scans each insertion point separately.

23
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:42,000
Once you've added the insertion points, right-click the request and choose Scan Defined Insertion Point.

24
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:48,000
Configure your scan options and check the dashboard for the results.

25
00:02:52,000 --> 00:03:00,000
In a similar way, you can also target a specific substring within a value, which can be useful for scanning non-standard data structures.

26
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:12,000
Supposing you have an application where the username contains a dash, for example, numbers-name, and the two parts could be seen as separate data points by the target application.

27
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:18,000
You can use Burp Intruder to define these as separate payloads without compromising the structure of the data.

28
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:28,000
As in the previous example, once you've defined the two insertion points, you can right-click and select Scan Defined Insertion Points.

29
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:35,000
After performing your scans, you can send requests to Burp Organizer if there are any you want to investigate at a later date,

30
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:39,000
without having to scan or browse through your target application again.

31
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:45,000
To do this, right-click a request and select Send to Organizer.

